<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Maclean</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Maclean was a Scottish officer who, as president of the council of merchants at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a> from 1830, governed the British forts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>. He made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> in 1831 and extended informal British jurisdiction over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> states, which led to the Bond of 1844.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-maclean.jpg" alt="George Maclean" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Maclean.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The 1831 Treaty of Peace, negotiated by <b>George Maclean</b>, recognised the River Pra as the effective southern boundary of Asante influence, permanently curtailing the empire's ambitions on the coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1830 Captain <b>George Maclean</b>, president of a council of merchants, made peace with Asante in 1831 and held court at Cape Coast; in his thirteen years there were no confrontations and trade reportedly trebled.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
